{"error":false,"activeTest":false,"task":{"id":14284125,"type":"Review","status":"Processed","importance":1,"reason":null,"locked":null,"proofId":null,"currentQuestionId":null,"currentQuestionTimestamp":null,"progress":1,"points":5,"passed":1,"rawPointsAwarded":7,"pointsAwarded":7,"mp":null,"mpAwarded":null,"mpDecayRate":null,"student":{"id":57975,"firstName":"Harrison","lastName":"Charles","locale":null,"subscriptionId":"sub_1UO3yHD4gcTFRx97dYjQXmX1","sequenceId":19},"course":{"id":136,"name":"Mathematical Foundations III"},"topic":{"id":463,"name":"L'Hopital's Rule"},"test":{"id":null,"name":null,"diagnostic":null,"timeLimit":null,"questionCount":null},"created":"2026-10-17T13:57:32.000Z","started":"2026-10-17T14:31:31.000Z","completed":"2026-10-17T14:32:17.000Z","processed":"2026-10-17T14:32:34.000Z","progressStr":"100%","answers":[{"id":62456069,"created":"2026-10-17T14:31:44.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12336,"number":1,"createdStr":"Sat, Oct 17th @ 2:31 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:12","question":{"id":31272,"format":"MC","difficulty":3,"exampleId":301,"text":"<p>${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{x^2} =}$</p>\n","choice1":"$-\\dfrac 1 {16} $","choice2":"$\\infty$ ","choice3":"$4$","choice4":"$-1$ ","choice5":"$-\\dfrac 14 $","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>If we plug in $x = 0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L&#39;Hopital&#39;s rule to find the limit:\n\\begin{align}\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4} - x - 8}{x^2} & = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\left[4\\sqrt{x+4} - x - 8\\right]'}{[x^2]'}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left[\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x + 4}} - 1 \\right]}{2x}\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>This time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L&#39;Hopital&#39;s rule once again. We get\n\\begin{align}\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4} - x - 8}{x^2} & = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left[\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x + 4}} - 1 \\right]}{2x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left[\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x + 4}} - 1 \\right]'}{[2x]'}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left[- \\dfrac{1}{(x+4)\\sqrt{x+4}} \\right]}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\dfrac{\\left[- \\dfrac{1}{(0+4)\\sqrt{0+4}} \\right]}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\dfrac{\\left[- \\dfrac{1}{4(2)} \\right]}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -\\dfrac{1}{16}.\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":463,"name":"L'Hopital's Rule"},"example":{"id":301,"title":"Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"},"group":{"id":96436,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[{"id":null,"number":null,"answers":[]}],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14284125,"answer":{"id":62456069,"cread":"2026-10-17T14:31:44.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12336},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyHard","difficultySymbol":"H","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62456076,"created":"2026-10-17T14:31:58.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":10051,"number":2,"createdStr":"Sat, Oct 17th @ 2:31 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:10","question":{"id":31597,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":303,"text":"<p>${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {2\\ln x}{e^x} =}$</p>\n","choice1":"$0$ ","choice2":"$\\infty$","choice3":"$1$","choice4":"$2$ ","choice5":"$-1$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ so applying  L&#39;Hopital&#39;s rule yields\n\\begin{align}\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {2\\ln x}{e^x} & = \\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac {\\left[2\\ln x\\right]'}{[e^x]'}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x}\\right)}{e^x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{xe^x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = 0.\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":463,"name":"L'Hopital's Rule"},"example":{"id":303,"title":"Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"},"group":{"id":96458,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14284125,"answer":{"id":62456076,"cread":"2026-10-17T14:31:58.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":10051},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62456094,"created":"2026-10-17T14:32:17.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15422,"number":3,"createdStr":"Sat, Oct 17th @ 2:32 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:15","question":{"id":72336,"format":"MC","difficulty":2,"exampleId":302,"text":"<p>Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4} - 1\\right)} .$</p>\n","choice1":"$0$","choice2":"$\\infty$","choice3":"$\\dfrac{1}{2}$","choice4":"$\\dfrac{1}{4}$","choice5":"$1$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2} = \\infty$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4} - 1\\right) = \\infty,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}.$</p>\n<p>Applying L&#39;Hopital&#39;s rule, we obtain</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\\lim_{x \\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4} - 1\\right)} & = \\lim_{x \\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left[\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right]'}{\\left[\\dfrac{1}{x^4} - 1\\right]'}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\lim_{x \\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{x^3}\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac{4}{x^5} \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{2x^{-3}}{4x^{-5}}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = 0.\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":463,"name":"L'Hopital's Rule"},"example":{"id":302,"title":"Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"},"group":{"id":96444,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14284125,"answer":{"id":62456094,"cread":"2026-10-17T14:32:17.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":15422},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyModerate","difficultySymbol":"M","rendered":true,"testMode":false}}],"completedStr":"Completed @ 2:32 PM","localGraph":{"topicId":463,"topics":[{"id":281,"name":"Second and Higher-Order Derivatives","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":2.799880683583528,"repetition":2,"stability":0.23934582822304215},{"id":463,"name":"L'Hopital's Rule","prerequisites":[281,1115,1377,1717,1905,1958,1986],"frontier":0,"halfLife":0.6756207052791169,"repetition":1,"stability":0.1362840882590144},{"id":1115,"name":"Selecting Procedures for Calculating Derivatives","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":3.849896261774474,"repetition":2,"stability":0.269783206511139},{"id":1210,"name":"L'Hopital's Rule Applied to Tables","prerequisites":[463]},{"id":1377,"name":"Limits of Logarithmic Functions","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":1.9872812852140802,"repetition":2,"stability":0.21006829847281305},{"id":1717,"name":"Limits of Exponential Functions","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":0.7911523099042538,"repetition":1,"stability":0.14567708167990787},{"id":1741,"name":"Limits of Vector-Valued Functions","prerequisites":[463]},{"id":1905,"name":"Limits of Reciprocal Functions","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":2.1058819078123836,"repetition":2,"stability":0.2147857301957855},{"id":1958,"name":"Limits of Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":0.7512885847519241,"repetition":1,"stability":0.14255744214459926},{"id":1986,"name":"Limits of Radical Functions ","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":4.535442249977052,"repetition":2,"stability":0.2868040589941007},{"id":2156,"name":"Little-O Notation","prerequisites":[463]},{"id":2854,"name":"Big-O Notat